From: Davidlohr Bueso <dave@stgolabs.net>
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] nvme-pci: cap the default transfer size at the DMA-optimal size

Commit 3710e2b056cb ("nvme-pci: clamp max_hw_sectors based on DMA
optimized limitation") capped the transfer size at
dma_opt_mapping_size() so that per-IO IOVA allocations stay within
the IOVA range cache: on a translated IOMMU domain that is 128KB
(the largest rcache size class), and anything larger allocates and
frees its IOVA through the global rbtree lock, which was the
scalability wall the clamp avoided.

The cost model inverted when
commit 7ce3c1dd78fc ("nvme-pci: convert the data mapping to blk_rq_dma_map")
moved nvme-pci onto
commit 858299dc6160 ("block: add scatterlist-less DMA mapping helpers"):
a request is now mapped with a single IOVA allocation covering the
whole transfer, not with per-segment allocations proportional to its
size, so larger transfers cost fewer mapping operations, not more,
and the rbtree allocation rate is bounded by device bandwidth rather
than IOPS.

Larger transfers are a measurable win where per-command overhead
dominates: on a 16-vCPU KVM guest with a virtual VT-d and a
RAM-backed emulated NVMe (4MB MDTS), lifting the 128KB cap takes 2MB
sequential reads from 7.5 to 17.6 GiB/s (+133%) and 512K random
reads from 7.3 to 13.8 GiB/s (+89%), with 4K random unchanged. A
second reproduction on a 14-vCPU guest (EPYC 9124 host, emulated
VT-d, RAM-backed NVMe, 4MB MDTS) measures +371% and +63% for the
same two shapes through the max_sectors_kb opt-in this patch adds,
4K again unchanged. Interrupt and completion counts drop
proportionally on bare metal too (-94% interrupts/GB for 2MB reads
on an EPYC 9124 host, AMD-Vi translated lazy domain, Samsung PM9A3).

They are not a win everywhere: on that same bare metal host the
PM9A3 serves one 2MB command with less internal parallelism than
sixteen 128KB commands, so 2MB sequential reads lose 8% at moderate
queue depth (deeper queues do not recover it), and 4K readers
competing with unsplit 2MB streams see their p99 latency go from
4.6ms to 11.1ms. Re-capping the request size via max_sectors_kb
restores base behavior exactly, confirming the delta is purely
request-size policy, not mapping cost.

Express exactly that split: bound max_hw_sectors only by the
driver's descriptor sizing (NVME_MAX_BYTES) and dma_max_mapping_size()
(a real limit only for bounce-buffered untrusted devices), and apply
dma_opt_mapping_size() to the default max_sectors via the new
queue_limits.max_default_sectors instead. Default behavior on
translated domains is unchanged - requests still split at 128KB -
but administrators can now raise max_sectors_kb up to the MDTS bound
for the deployments where larger commands win (virtualized NVMe,
interrupt- or power-constrained hosts), a choice the hard cap
previously denied. Domains without a genuine DMA optimum
(dma_opt == dma_max: direct DMA, passthrough) keep today's defaults,
as do the non-PCI transports, which leave opt_transfer_sectors at 0.

Also drop the stale "iod->sg allocations" wording while at it:
nvme-pci has not used scatterlists since the blk_rq_dma_map
conversion.

Signed-off-by: Davidlohr Bueso <dave@stgolabs.net>
---
 drivers/nvme/host/core.c |  1 +
 drivers/nvme/host/nvme.h |  1 +
 drivers/nvme/host/pci.c  | 25 ++++++++++++++++++++++---
 3 files changed, 24 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/nvme/host/core.c b/drivers/nvme/host/core.c
index 453c1f0b2dd0..dc3b56a06773 100644
--- a/drivers/nvme/host/core.c
+++ b/drivers/nvme/host/core.c
@@ -2066,6 +2066,7 @@ static void nvme_set_ctrl_limits(struct nvme_ctrl *ctrl,
 		struct queue_limits *lim, bool is_admin)
 {
 	lim->max_hw_sectors = ctrl->max_hw_sectors;
+	lim->max_default_sectors = ctrl->opt_transfer_sectors;
 	lim->max_segments = min_t(u32, USHRT_MAX,
 		min_not_zero(nvme_max_drv_segments(ctrl), ctrl->max_segments));
 	lim->max_integrity_segments = ctrl->max_integrity_segments;
diff --git a/drivers/nvme/host/nvme.h b/drivers/nvme/host/nvme.h
index 824651cc898d..653d05638db7 100644
--- a/drivers/nvme/host/nvme.h
+++ b/drivers/nvme/host/nvme.h
@@ -375,6 +375,7 @@ struct nvme_ctrl {
 
 	u64 cap;
 	u32 max_hw_sectors;
+	u32 opt_transfer_sectors;
 	u32 max_segments;
 	u32 max_integrity_segments;
 	u32 max_zeroes_sectors;
diff --git a/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c b/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c
index 69932d640b53..03e8939cfe1e 100644
--- a/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c
+++ b/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c
@@ -3726,12 +3726,32 @@ static struct nvme_dev *nvme_pci_alloc_dev(struct pci_dev *pdev,
 	dma_set_max_seg_size(&pdev->dev, 0xffffffff);
 
 	/*
-	 * Limit the max command size to prevent iod->sg allocations going
-	 * over a single page.
+	 * Bound the max command size by the driver's descriptor sizing
+	 * (NVME_MAX_BYTES) and by what the DMA API can map in one call,
+	 * a real limit only for bounce-buffered (untrusted) devices.
+	 * Requests are mapped with a single IOVA allocation, not per
+	 * segment, so larger transfers cost fewer mapping operations,
+	 * not more.
 	 */
 	dev->ctrl.max_hw_sectors = min_t(u32,
 			NVME_MAX_BYTES >> SECTOR_SHIFT,
-			dma_opt_mapping_size(&pdev->dev) >> 9);
+			dma_max_mapping_size(&pdev->dev) >> 9);
+
+	/*
+	 * On translated IOMMU domains, transfers above the DMA-optimal
+	 * mapping size (the IOVA range-cache bound, 128KB on x86)
+	 * allocate their IOVA through the global rbtree lock, and some
+	 * devices extract less internal parallelism from fewer, larger
+	 * commands.  Keep the default request size capped at the optimal
+	 * size, but leave max_hw_sectors as the true ceiling so that
+	 * administrators can raise max_sectors_kb where larger commands
+	 * win (fewer completions, interrupts and mapping operations).
+	 * dma_opt == dma_max means no genuine hint exists (direct DMA or
+	 * passthrough); leave the default alone there.
+	 */
+	if (dma_opt_mapping_size(&pdev->dev) < dma_max_mapping_size(&pdev->dev))
+		dev->ctrl.opt_transfer_sectors =
+			dma_opt_mapping_size(&pdev->dev) >> 9;
 	dev->ctrl.max_segments = NVME_MAX_SEGS;
 	dev->ctrl.max_integrity_segments = 1;
 	return dev;
--
